# encoder_i16_fwd_2026-08-02 — provenance
# Companion to encoder_i16_fwd_2026-08-02.md, its .ab.tsv, and the audit output
# i16_fwd_audit_2026-08-02.txt.
#
# This is a CHANGE + its before/after measurement, not a profiling session:
# lever 2 of benchmarks/encoder_hotspot_reprofile_2026-08-02.md ("bd8 i16-lane
# FORWARD transform", +19.63 ms / 16.6 % of the gap projected). That ceiling is
# ARITHMETIC ON A MEASURED SELF-COST, not a forecast of a change; this file is
# what the change actually returned.

date                 2026-08-02
box                  Apple M4 Pro, 12 cores, 24 GiB, macOS 26.5.2 (Darwin arm64)
baseline commit      590e525f89185a15b201a8a77316db7e7e6d3940 (main at session start; the re-profile's scoping banner)
landing commits      0be3a43 (the i16 forward path), 7976c0f (gate the shift domains)
worktree             zenav1-aom--i16fwd, detached
libaom oracle        upstream/ submodule 03087864cf4bea6abb0d28f95cf7843511413d8f (v3.14.1)
rustc                see `rustc -vV` in the log; stable toolchain
build flags          --release, NO -C target-cpu=native (runtime SIMD dispatch, what users get)
threading            single-threaded on every arm
nice                 every measured run under `nice -n 19`

## Profile cell — unchanged from all three prior encoder studies, for continuity

1024x1024 photo, cq-level 44, cpu-used 6, ALLINTRA (usage 2), 8-bit 4:2:0,
single tile, single thread. Source $HOME/tmp/xb/src/photo_1024.yuv (the file the
2026-08-01 cross-encoder study, both encoder profiles and the allocation
landing used). Every arm emits the same 4472-byte stream at this cell —
verified by sha, not by byte count alone.

## Arms

Six port arms plus the C oracle, ONE binary each, all built from this one
worktree by patching the two gate sites and restoring from git
(~/tmp/i16fwd/build_arms.sh; sha-verified distinct):

  base    590e525f89185a15b201a8a77316db7e7e6d3940, both gates absent
  col     the i16 COLUMN pass only (the row gate short-circuited)
  row     the i16 ROW pass only (the column gate short-circuited)
  both    7976c0f as it ships
  bothB   a SECOND COPY of the identical `both` binary — the box's noise floor,
          measured rather than asserted (docs/DIFFERENTIAL_PLAYBOOK.md §6)
  baseB   likewise on the PRE side, so the floor is measured at both ends
  libaom-c  benchmarks/xbench/csrc/drv_libaom.c against the pinned oracle

A second band (.halfbatch.tsv) compares `both` against a `half` arm that also
runs 8-wide / 8-tall blocks as HALF batches, plus its own null. That extension
measured NULL and was reverted; see the .md.

The call-routing census in the .md ("(b) Half the forward-transform calls are
structurally ineligible") came from a TEMPORARY atomic counter in
try_fwd_col_pass / try_fwd_row_pass plus a throwaway
crates/aom-bench/examples/fwd_i16_reach.rs, both removed before commit. Neither
is in the tree; the numbers are one run of the profile cell.

The two levers are measured separately even though they ship together, per
§14's closing rule: `col` and `row` bite on different passes and there is no
reason to assume they are additive.

## Exact commands

  git worktree add --detach ~/work/zen/zenav1-aom--i16fwd origin/main
  git -C ~/work/zen/zenav1-aom--i16fwd submodule update --init

  # the C oracle driver (the prebuilt libaom.a comes from the submodule build)
  nice -n 19 cc -O2 -o benchmarks/xbench/target/drv_libaom \
      benchmarks/xbench/csrc/drv_libaom.c -Iupstream -Iupstream/build \
      -Lupstream/build -laom -lm -lc++

  bash ~/tmp/i16fwd/build_arms.sh          # the port arms (base/col/row/both)
  cp ~/tmp/i16fwd/drv-base ~/tmp/i16fwd/drv-baseB   # the two null arms
  cp ~/tmp/i16fwd/drv-both ~/tmp/i16fwd/drv-bothB

  nice -n 19 bash scripts/eprof_ab.sh 24 <out.tsv> \
      base=~/tmp/i16fwd/drv-base baseB=~/tmp/i16fwd/drv-baseB \
      col=~/tmp/i16fwd/drv-col row=~/tmp/i16fwd/drv-row \
      both=~/tmp/i16fwd/drv-both bothB=~/tmp/i16fwd/drv-bothB \
      libaom-c=~/tmp/i16fwd/drv-libaom-c
  python3 scripts/eprof_ab_stats.py <out.tsv> --ref libaom-c --vs base

## The audit

  just audit-i16-fwd            # = python3 xtask/audit_i16_fwd.py --cells
  just gen-txfm1d               # regenerates fwd1d_v3_i16_gen.rs (reproduces byte-identically)

`xtask/audit_i16_fwd.py` is deterministic and needs nothing but the in-tree
kernel sources, so every number in the .md's audit table is re-derivable by
re-running it. `reference/extracted/` is gitignored; the six forward kernel
bodies are re-extractable from `upstream/av1/encoder/av1_fwd_txfm1d.c` (the
scalar regeneration was diffed against the committed `txfm1d_gen.rs` and
differs only in the two `use` paths, which is the check that the extraction is
the same one the committed kernels came from).

## Byte-identity gate

  AOM_CONFORMANCE_DIR=<primary checkout>/conformance/data \
    cargo nextest run --cargo-profile test-fast -j 4 --workspace \
      --no-fail-fast --run-ignored all                     # SIMD dispatch live
  AOM_FORCE_SCALAR=1 <same>                                # scalar-pinned
  cargo check --target x86_64-apple-darwin --workspace --all-targets

`--run-ignored all` is included deliberately (the KB-PERF-1 session found two
stale pins that way). The conformance corpus must be present or ~40 tests
fail-loud with "conformance vector missing" — that is the fail-loud design
working, not a regression; this worktree borrows the primary checkout's
gitignored `conformance/data` READ-ONLY via `AOM_CONFORMANCE_DIR`.

## Known gaps

* ONE cell, one image, one content class. cpu-used 3/4/5/9 are not measured
  here — the re-profile records that 9 (5.64x) and 4 (7.76x) are worse cells
  and were never decomposed.
* 8-bit 4:2:0 only. The i16 path's gate is a runtime bound so bd10/bd12 blocks
  simply decline it, but that decline is not measured for cost.
* Wall clock only. No instruction count (no valgrind on Apple Silicon), so
  "half the lane width" and "less time" are two different measurements and only
  the second is what the ratio moved on.
* No multi-threaded measurement; neither arm is threaded.
* x86-64 / Windows IS measured — see the .md. GitHub Actions run 30788058276,
  `.github/workflows/winperf.yml` with `arms: prepost`, `base_sha`
  590e525f89185a15b201a8a77316db7e7e6d3940, `rounds: 16`; the four bands are
  committed as `.win_<runner>_<content>.tsv`. What is NOT measured there is
  this box's photograph: winperf generates its sources with integer-only
  arithmetic (so the census is bit-identical across targets) and cannot ship
  the 1.5 MB study image, so the platform comparison is confounded with content
  and only its ordering is claimed.
* Linux is unmeasured on both the audit and the timing.

## Committed data files

  .ab.tsv         the 24-round interleaved band, 7 arms x 24 rounds
  .halfbatch.tsv  the 24-round band for the rejected half-batch extension
  .win_<runner>_<content>.tsv   the four Windows bands (16 rounds, 5 arms)
  i16_fwd_audit_2026-08-02.txt   `just audit-i16-fwd` output (M* + bd8 reach)
